CONSPECTUS: Since the first report on the long-term durable $9.7\%$ solid-state perovskite solar cell employing methylammonium lead iodide $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\right)$ , mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ and $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis $\cdot_{N,N}$ -di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino] $]-9,9^{\prime}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-MeOTAD) in 2012, following the seed technologies on perovskitesensitized liquid junction solar cells in 2009 and 2011, a surge of interest has been focused on perovskite solar cells due to superb photovoltaic performance and extremely facile fabrication processes. The power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells reached $21\%$ in a very short period of time. Such an unprecedentedly high photovoltaic performance is due to the intrinsic optoelectronic property of organolead iodide perovskite material. Moreover, a high dielectric constant, sub-millimeter scale carrier diffusion length, an underlying ferroelectric property, and ion migration behavior can make organolead halide perovskites suitable for multifunctionality. Thus, besides solar cell applications, perovskite material has recently been applied to a variety fields of materials science such as photodetectors, light emitting diodes, lasing, X-ray imaging, resistive memory, and water splitting. Regardless of application areas, the growth of a well-defined perovskite layer with high crystallinity is essential for effective utilization of its excellent physicochemical properties. Therefore, an effective methodology for preparation of high quality perovskite layers is required.
In this Account, an effective methodology for production of high quality perovskite layers is described, which is the Lewis acid− base adduct approach. In the solution process to form the perovskite layer, the key chemicals of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ (or $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{I})$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ are used by dissolving them in polar aprotic solvents. Since polar aprotic solvents bear oxygen, sulfur, or nitrogen, they can act as a Lewis base. In addition, the main group compound $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is known to be a Lewis acid. Thus, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ has a chance to form an adduct by reacting with the Lewis base. Crystal growth and morphology of perovskite can be controlled by taking advantage of the weak chemical interaction in the adduct. We have successfully fabricated highly reproducible $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells with PCE as high as $19.7\%$ via adducts of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with oxygen-donor $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethyl sulfoxide. This adduct approach has been found to be generally adopted, where formamidinium lead iodide perovskite, $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\right)$ , with large grain, high crystallinity, and long-lived carrier lifetime was successfully fabricated via an adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with sulfur-donor thiourea as Lewis base. The adduct approach proposed in this Account is a very promising methodology to achieve high quality perovskite films with high photovoltaic performance. Furthermore, single crystal growth on the conductive substrate is expected to be possible if we kinetically control the elimination of Lewis base in the adduct.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
A surge of recent progress in perovskite solar cells originated from the breakthrough technology of the long-term durable solid-state perovskite solar cell reported in 2012 by the groups of Park and Snaith following two important seed studies on perovskite sensitized liquid junction solar cells reported in 2009 by Miyasaka’s group and in 2011 by Park’s group.1−4 Thanks to the outstanding intrinsic properties of organolead halide perovskites, a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $20.1\%$ was certified by optimizing the fabrication process and composition.5 More recently, an improved PCE of $21\%$ was certified by NREL (http://www.nrel.gov/ncpv/images/ efficiency_chart.jpg). However, despite such superb photovoltaic performance, moisture and thermal stability and current−voltage hysteresis are still critical issues to be addressed. Since organolead halide perovskite is an ionic crystal, the perovskite light absorber has been reported to be vulnerable to light, humidity, and heat in air.6−8 Encapsulation of the perovskite solar cell might protect the perovskite light absorber from air and humidity.9,10 Chemical modifications of perovskite materials have been developed to enhance its intrinsic stability against light and humidity.11−14 Besides stability, mismatch of current−voltage curves due to scandirection-dependent capacitive current is another issue to be solved.15,16
Apart from the solar cell application, organolead halide perovskites show excellent multifunctional characteristics in a variety of the fields such as photodetectors, light emitting diodes, lasing, X-ray imaging, and water splitting,1 7−21 which is basically due to the unprecedentedly high absorption coefficient, micrometer scale carrier diffusion length, and high photoluminescence (PL) quantum yield.18,22,23
Regardless of application areas, growth of a well-defined perovskite layer with high crystallinity is regarded as an essential for effective utilization of its excellent properties. The charge carrier diffusion length of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ was reported to vary from several hundreds of nanometers to several hundreds of micrometers, depending on trap density.22−25 Density of trap states resulting from defects and grain boundaries was found to hinder the free charge carrier diffusion, leading to nonradiative recombination and thereby reducing seriously the charge carrier lifetime and PL yield.26,27 Millimeter sized free-standing single crystals of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and MAPbBr3 grown in solution23,24 are expected to improve significantly carrier lifetime and radiative recombination; however, these may be hard to apply to realistic devices. Therefore, an efficient method to grow single-crystal-like perovskite on a desired substrate is required.
In this Account, we report on a Lewis acid−base adduct approach for high efficiency perovskite solar cell fabrication. Regarding solution-processed perovskite formation, selection of solvents to dissolve $\mathrm{Pb}X_{2}$ $\mathrm{\boldmath~X=I},$ Br, Cl) and organic iodide is important. Most solvents, such as N,N-dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), and $\gamma$ -butylolactone (GBL), are found to be polar aprotic, which indicates that those solvents can act as Lewis bases. We have successfully fabricated highly reproducible $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells with PCE as high as $19.7\%$ via an adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with oxygendonor DMSO.28 The adduct approach has been found to be adopted generally, where formamidinium lead iodide perovskite $[\mathrm{H}\bar{\mathrm{C}}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\ (\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})]$ with large grain, high crystallinity, and long-lived carrier lifetime is successfully fabricated via an adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with thiourea as sulfur-donor Lewis base.
2. LEWIS ACID−BASE ADDUCT AND $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ PEROVSKITE SOLAR CELL VIA $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ ADDUCT WITH $o$ -DONOR DMSO
In the Lewis acid−base concept,29 a base is defined as electronpair donor and an acid as electron-pair acceptor. This definition can expand to acids including metal ions and main group compounds. Lewis acid−base reaction provides a framework for nonaqueous reaction. Reaction of a Lewis acid with a Lewis base leads to either redox (reduction or oxidation) reaction or adduct formation, where the adduct is linked by a dative bond (shared electrons are originated from the Lewis base) to form a kind of coordination compound. The $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ halides are known to be Lewis acids because they accept iodide anion to form iodoplumbate anions such as $[\mathrm{\tilde{Pb}_{3}I_{10}}]^{4-}$ and $[\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16}]^{6-}$ .30,31 It was reported that lead(II) halides $\left(\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{2}\right)$ are able to form 1:1, 1:2, or 2:1 adducts with monodentate (L) and bidentate (LL) ligands with oxygen, sulfur, and nitrogen donors.32 Figure 1 depicts some examples of Lewis bases in terms of donor type (O-donor, S-donor, and $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -donor), where DMSO and $N\cdot$ - methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) are $o$ -donor bases, thiourea and thioacetamide are S-donor bases, and pyridine and aniline are N-donor bases.
$\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ with high charge extraction ability was reported to be prepared via the adduct approach.28 To induce a 1:1 adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ DMSO was chosen as a Lewis base. During dissolution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in DMF, equimolar DMSO was added. An adduct of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DM}$ SO is formed in the presence of DMSO, which eventually leads to a highly uniform $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ layer as can be seen in Figure 2. On the other hand, the solution without DMSO results in the rod shaped $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ crystals that do not fully cover the substrate. This indicates that the intermediate adduct plays a crucial role in controlling the morphology of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . If the formation rate constant for perovskite is high, crystal growth is hard to control. Crystal growth via the Lewis acid−base adduct is expected to be homogeneously controlled because the removal process of the Lewis base in the adduct will retard the formation rate constant. The equilibrium constant for adduct formation depends on Lewis basicity. DMF can also act as Lewis base to form an adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ because it contains lone-pair electrons on oxygen. However, DMSO is expected to have a larger formation constant than DMF because of stronger Lewis basicity due to the electron donating methyl group in DMSO. Thus, DMSO is likely to form an adduct with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in a competing reaction. Adduct formation can be confirmed by infrared (IR) spectroscopy. 28,32
3. $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ PEROVSKITE SOLAR CELL VIA $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ ADDUCT WITH S-DONOR THIOUREA
The adduct approach has been extended to $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ . When choosing the Lewis base for the adduct formation, we paid attention to the structure of the organic cation in the lead halide perovskite. For the case of $\mathrm{\bfMAPbI}_{3},$ DMSO may be a good base because it has common functional group of ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ . In the case of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3},$ the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ functional group in the Lewis base is considered because two $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ groups are included in $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ . It was also reported that S-donors were stronger than O-donors.32 From this design rule, thiourea is selected to form an adduct with $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{i}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ because it contains both a S-donor and an $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functional group.
Figure 3 shows the IR spectra for DMSO (liquid), thiourea (powder), and their adducts (powder; see Supporting Information for details about preparation). Figure 3a,b shows the IR spectra of bare DMSO, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}_{\cdot}$ , and FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · DMSO. The stretching vibration of $s{=}0$ ( $\scriptstyle\left(\nu(S=\mathrm{O})\right)$ ) appears at $1045~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for bare $\mathrm{DMSO}^{33}$ and is shifted to $1019~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ upon formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}$ . The shift of the $s{=}0$ vibration in DMSO to lower wavenumber upon interaction with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is indicative of formation of an adduct, since it is a consequence of a weakened $s{=}0$ bond strength caused by interaction with the Lewis acid $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .28,34 The $s{=}0$ vibration is, however, hardly shifted when FAI is added, which indicates interaction between FAI and DMSO is very weak or negligible. The IR spectra of thiourea, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea, and $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · thiourea are presented in Figure 3d,e. The stretching vibration of $s{=}C$ appears at $730~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for bare thiourea35 and shows progressive shift to lower wavenumber of 724 and $714~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ upon reaction with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ alone and FAI and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ respectively, indicative of formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea and FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea adducts. The progressive shift of $s{=}C$ vibration to lower wavenumber indicates that thiourea strongly interacts with both $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and FAI. Figure 3c,f shows as-spin-coated FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · DMSO and $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ ·thiourea films on a glass substrate. The diethyl ether washed intermediate film is opaque for DMSObased $\mathrm{FAI-PbI}_{2}$ film, probably due to the presence of noninteracting FAI in the film, whereas the thiourea-containing film forms the FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea adduct as evidenced by highly transparent characteristics, as was the transparent adduct of MAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·DMSO film.28 Although the 1:1 adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and thiourea has been reported previously, the stoichiometry of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·FAI·thiourea adduct is still unclear.32 However, when considering the transparent film formed after spin-coating, shift of $s{=}C$ stretching vibration energy as confirmed by FTIR, and nominal composition of starting materials, a 1:1:1 adduct of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ FAI, and thiourea is likely to be formed.
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) for FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·DMSO and FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea powders is performed in Figure 4. As can be seen from the TG spectrum of $\mathrm{\mathrm{4AI{\cdot}P b I_{2}{\cdot}D M S O}}$ in Figure $\mathbf{4a},$ DMSO starts to rapidly escape from the powder at $70.3^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and the loss of DMSO might be completed at around $^{170.8}\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ considering its weight loss by $11\%$ in FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·DMSO. Additional weight loss from 170.8 to $331.9~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can be assigned to loss of FAI.14,36 For the case of FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea in Figure $^{4\mathrm{b},}$ the weight loss starts from relatively higher temperature of $175.4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which can be assigned to loss of thiourea.
Considering its weight ratio of 10.7 wt $\%$ in $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea, loss of thiourea is completed at around $230~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Notably, an inflection point at $170.8\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ observed from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·DMSO powder resulting from loss of FAI is not observed for the FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea case, while weight loss start at $329.6\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can be assigned to loss of FAI. As can be seen in Figure 4, bare thiourea starts to be eliminated at higher temperature (188.2 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\backslash$ than DMSO $(86~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Therefore, the elimination temperature of Lewis base in adduct is not related to the extent of interaction with Lewis acids.
Figure 5 shows plane-view scanning electron microscopic (SEM) images of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ film prepared from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}D M S O_{1-x}}$ thioureax) adducts along with elemental analysis using energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). For making FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\mathrm{\DMSO}_{1-x}$ thioureax) solution, the corresponding amount of precursor solutions for $\mathrm{FAI{\cdot}P b I_{2}{\cdot}D M S O}$ and FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea were mixed. From EDS analysis in Figure $\mathsf{S a},\mathsf{b},$ sulfur detected from as-spin-coated FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea before heat treatment is completely removed after heat-treatment at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The removal of sulfur is also confirmed from the difference in normalized EDS spectra in the inset of Figure 5c (energy ranges from 2 to $3\mathrm{keV}_{\cdot}$ , where main peaks for lead and sulfur are observed), in which the peak at $2.31\mathrm{\keV}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ peak for sulfur $(2.307~\mathrm{eV})$ is distinctly observed. For the case of the $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ heat-treated $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ film via FA $\mathrm{I}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMSO}$ $\left(x=0\right)$ ), domains whose size ranging from $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $1\mu\mathrm m$ are randomly distributed (Figure 5c). As the $x$ (the amount of thiourea) increases, the domain size is significantly increased, in which the size ranges from 1 to 4 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ for $x=0.2$ and from 4 to $7\mu\mathrm{m}$ for $x=0.4$ , 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0 (Figures 5d−i. However, as the thiourea content increases from $x=0.4$ to $x=1.0\$ , the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer uncovered with $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ starts to appear and becomes pronounced. Increase in the uncovered compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with increasing thiourea content indicates that the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ crystal grows three-dimensionally, which also creates a large gap between $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ crystals. Small tiny particles on the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ appear as thiourea content increases, which might be an unknown byproduct that will be discussed in X-ray diffraction study.
Figure 6a shows absorption spectra of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ films prepared from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\left(\mathrm{DMSO}_{1-x}\mathrm{thiourea}_{x}\right)$ adducts. With $x\ =\ 0.2,$ absorption of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ film is highly enhanced over the whole wavelength region. The enhancement of absorption might result from the increased crystal size, which can lead to higher light harvesting efficiency as reported previously.37,38 The absorption at the band edge region is further enhanced at higher content of thiourea $\stackrel{\cdot}{x}=0.\dot{6}$ , 0.8, and 1.0), while the absorption at shorter wavelength regions $\mathrm{\from}\ 300$ to $640\mathrm{nm}$ ) is decreased for the $x$ equal to and higher than 0.4. $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction patterns of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ films prepared from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · DMSO $\left(x\overset{\_}{=}0\right)$ , $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\left(\mathrm{DMSO}_{0.8}\mathrm{thiourea}_{0.2}\right)$ $\left(x=0.2\right)$ ), and FAI $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·thiourea $\left(x=1.0\right)$ are presented in Figure 6b. Overall, intensity and full-width half-maximum (fwhm) of peaks originating from black phase $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ are highly enhanced when thiourea is added and increased (see Figure 6c for $x=$ 1.0), which is correlated to significantly increased domain size of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ as confirmed by SEM in Figure 5. However, it is hard to confirm that the observed domain in Figure 5 is a single grain. Average crystallite size calculated from XRD measurement using the Scherrer equation is determined to be $50.26\ \mathrm{nm}$ for x = 0, 119.18 nm for x = 0.2, and 115.83 nm for x = 1.0.39 The crystallite size is significantly increased by $137\%$ after addition of $20\%$ thiourea, which indicates that the increased domain size for $x=0.2$ is probably attributed to the increased grain size. Although we confirm the absence of thiourea using EDS analysis (Figure 5), unknown XRD peaks are detected for $x=0.2$ and $x=1.0$ . Since the thiourea is reported to release $\mathrm{CS}_{2},$ $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfNCN}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ HNCS gases, possibly $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NCN}$ makes a new compound by reacting with $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{I}$ or $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ since the sulfur is not detected.40 Particularly, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas was reported to react with perovskite and bleach the absorption.41 Probably, the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas reacts with $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ and forms unknown compounds as byproducts.
Photovoltaic performances of perovskite solar cells incorporating $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ prepared from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\mathrm{'DMSO}_{1-x}\mathrm{thiourea}_{x})$ adduct are compared in Figure 7. All the data presented in the plots are mean values of the data measured at the reverse $\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ to $J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ and the forward $(J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ to $V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ scan. Addition of thiourea with $x=0.2$ improves short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ from $20.4\pm0.8$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0,$ to $21.5\pm0.4~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , corresponding to
$5.4\%$ increment, while further increase in thiourea content leads to decrease of average $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ to $21.0\pm0.3\$ ， $20.6\pm0.4$ , $20.2\pm0.3\$ , and $19.1~\pm~0.4~\mathrm{{\mA/cm}}^{2}$ for $x\ =\ 0.4,$ 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0, respectively. Average open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ is slightly enhanced from $0.896\pm0.032$ $\langle x=0\rangle$ to $0.903\pm0.053\mathrm{~V~}$ ( $\overset{\cdot}{x}=$ 0.2) and $0.922\pm0.034\mathrm{~V~}$ $\left(x=0.4\right)$ ) but declines to $0.892\pm$ 0.018, $0.858\ \pm\ 0.016$ , and $0.768\pm0.066\mathrm{~V~}$ for the higher thiourea content of $x=0.6$ , 0.8, and 1.0, respectively. Fill factor (FF) is improved by $20.9\%$ from $0.545\pm0.031$ $\left(x=0\right)$ ) to
$0.659\pm0.040$ $\mathit{\Psi}_{(x=0.2)}^{\prime}$ and by $22.6\%$ to $0.668\pm0.030$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Omega}}}$ 0.4). Higher content of thiourea, however, decreases FF to $0.662\pm0.022$ , $0.648\pm0.023$ , and $0.557\pm0.054$ for $x=0.6,$ 0.8, and 1.0, respectively.
Origin of change in $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ is investigated by external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra in Figure 8a. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values are calculated to be 16.4, 21.1, 20.7, 20.4, 19.5, and $18.5\ \mathrm{mA}/\$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for $x=0$ , 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0, respectively, which are well consistent with the tendency observed from $J{-}V$ measurement. The large discrepancy for $x=0$ is probably due to slow response of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ as can be seen in the time-dependent current density in Figure $^{8\mathrm{b}}$ ; on the other hand, a relatively lower discrepancy $(<4\%)$ for the presence of thiourea $\left(x\ge0.2\right)$ is due to fast response of $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ as evidenced by the timedependent current density in Figure 8b.14 The enhancement of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ for $x=0.2$ can be attributed to increased absorption as observed from UV−vis spectra in Figure 6a. A gradual decrease in EQE with thiourea content $\left(x\geq0.4\right)$ together with more pronounced decrease in the short wavelength region correlates with gradual decrease in absorption in the short wavelength region as $x$ increases. Steady-state current density and PCE for $x=0$ and 0.2 are presented in Figure 8c. Steady-state current density and PCE reach their maxima of $17.4\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and $13.6\%$ within $20~\mathsf{s}$ for $x=0.2$ , whereas it takes a longer time to reach maximum current density of $15.3\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and PCE of $11.5\%$ for $x=0$ .
Figure 9 presents cross-sectional SEM images of perovskite solar cells incorporating $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ prepared via FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·DMSO and $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ · $\mathrm{'DMSO}_{0.8}\mathrm{thiourea}_{0.2}\rangle$ adducts. Compared with small domains of about $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ and rough surface of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ film prepared without thiourea $(x=0)$ in Figure $\begin{array}{r}{9\mathsf{a},\mathsf{c},}\end{array}$ t h e $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ fi l m p r e p a r e d f r o m t h e $\mathrm{FAI}\cdot\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ · $\left(\mathrm{DMSO}_{0.8}\mathrm{thiourea}_{0.2}\right)$ ) adduct shows much larger domains $(\sim3\ \mu\mathrm{m})$ with highly smooth interface with $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis$[N,N]$ -di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiroMeOTAD) layer as can be seen in Figure $^{\mathrm{9b,d.}}$ . A larger domain consisting of larger perovskite crystals and smooth interface of the thiourea-induced $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ are responsible for the higher photovoltaic performance than the case without thiourea.
Figure $^{10\mathrm{a},\mathrm{b}}$ compares $J{-}V$ hysteresis, where hysteresis is significantly reduced for the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ prepared from $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · $\left(\mathrm{DMSO}_{0.8}\mathrm{thiourea}_{0.2}\right)$ compared with the one from $\mathrm{FAI}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ DMSO, which is already confirmed from steady-state current density measurement in Figure 8. Capacitance−frequency $\left(C-\right.$
$\hat{f})$ curves obtained using impedance spectroscopy by varying AC frequency from 1 MHz to $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at short circuit condition (Figures 10c,d). The low-frequency capacitance at around ${1\mathrm{Hz}},$ mainly responsible for the observed $J{-}V$ hysteresis,16 is slightly lowered in the dark and significantly reduced under illumination for the adduct with thiourea $\mathit{\Psi}(x\ =\ 0.2)$ . The thickness of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ layer was measured to be $323\pm38~\mathrm{nm}$ for $x=0$ and $340\pm30\mathrm{nm}$ for $x=0.2$ (Figure 9). The relatively huge difference in capacitance (more than 1 order of magnitude under illumination) compared with the negligible difference in thickness indicates that the effect of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ layer thickness on capacitance can be ruled out. This low-frequency capacitance is related to interfacial electrode polarization due to capacitive current accumulated at interfaces. Since the selective contacts in the planar heterojunction structure studied in this work are the same for $x=0$ and $x=0.2$ , the reduced capacitance for $x=0.2$ is probably associated with large grain size with flat interface as observed from the cross-sectional SEM images in Figure 9.
Steady-state and time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ films prepared from FAI· $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·(DMSO1−xthioureax) adduct are displayed in Figure 11. For $x=0.2$ , the steady-state PL spectrum in Figure 11a shows blue shift by $6\mathrm{nm}$ (from 813 to $807~\mathrm{nm}$ ) along with line width narrowing from 46 to 44 meV. Such a blue shift with line narrowing is attributed to decrease of spontaneous radiative recombination between trap states, which is probably the origin of decreased $J{-}V$ hysteresis and enhanced $\bar{V_{\mathrm{OC}}}$ C.14,42 The sharper PL nature for x = 0.2 than for $x=0$ is also correlated with sharper band edge absorption.26 Figure 11b compares time-resolved PL spectra for $x=0$ and $x=$ 0.2, in which both of the spectra can be fit to biexponential decay with a fast component $\stackrel{\prime}{\tau}_{1}=0.4$ ns for $x=0$ and $\tau_{1}=0.4$ ns for $x=0.2$ ) and a slow one ( $\stackrel{\prime}{\tau}_{2}=17.7$ ns for $x=0$ and 31.3 ns for $x=0.2$ ). The fast decay component can be assigned to a surface property, while the slow component corresponds to a bulk property.25 When $x$ changes from 0 to 0.2, a small decrease in PL intensity of the fast decay surface component is observed, which is due to much larger grain size associated with reduced surface state. The PL lifetime of the fast decay component is identical at $0.4~\mathrm{ns}_{;}$ whereas that of slow decay component is significantly increased by $77\%$ (from 17.7 to 31.3 ns). This implies that charge carrier lifetime in bulk perovskite is significantly improved for $x=0.2$ .22−26 The enhanced charge carrier lifetime with reduced trap density can explain the improved PCE along with less hysteresis for the thioureainduced $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ .
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
We have introduced in this Account a Lewis acid−base adduct approach as an effective way to control grain size and morphology of organolead iodide perovskite $\mathrm{APbI}_{3}$ $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{A}\ =\mathbf{\nabla}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{\bar{N}H}_{2})_{2},$ . Polar aprotic solvents used for the precursor solution containing organo iodide and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ were found to act as Lewis bases. DMSO was found to be an excellent intermediate for controlling morphology and grain size of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . For the formamidinium lead iodide $\left(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\right)$ , thiourea was better than DMSO because of the difference in chemical interaction. As the thiourea content increased, the domain size of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ increased substantially, up to $7\ \mu\mathrm{m},$ in which crystallite size increased by $137\%$ (from 50.26 to 115.83 nm). $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ formed via the adduct showed better photovoltaic performance because of faster charge transport and the reduced trap state. The adduct approach proposed in this Account is a very promising methodology to achieve high quality perovskite films with high photovoltaic performance.
